 agenda.local
X-PRUTS-SOURCE:thehmm
X-PRUTS-UUID:thehmm:10049-1792839600-1792861200@thehmm.nl
X-PRUTS-RADAR-ID:thehmm:10049-1792839600-1792861200@thehmm.nl
X-PRUTS-OCCURRENCE-ID:thehmm:10049-1792839600-1792861200@thehmm.nl:2026-10-
 24T11:00:00+02:00
DTSTAMP:20261010T020057Z
SUMMARY:Symposihmm 📡 Capture & Control
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
DTSTART:20261024T090000Z
DTEND:20261024T150000Z
DESCRIPTION:With our second Symposihmm of the year\, Capture & Control\, we
 ’ll be exploring the entanglements between processes of physiological an
 d geological capture. How can we make sense of this paranoid era in online
  platforms? And how are our minds and our planet being captured\, rendered
 \, and manipulated?\n\nIn the 2000s\, Stanford University taught ‘captol
 ogy’\, a course on computers as persuasive technologies. It’s perhaps
  no surprise that Mike Krieger\, who went on to create Instagram\, took th
 is class. Over the past twenty years\, social media platforms have continu
 ed to perfect the tools of persuasion. They have figured out with extreme
  precision how to grab and hold our attention\, while simultaneously becom
 ing the main source of information for many people. Following these shifts
  online\, artist and researcher Trevor Paglen has argued that these platfo
 rms are the perfect place for “influence operations”\, referring to th
 e psyops (psychological operations) of secret services. Whereas back in th
 e day no one on the internet knew you were a dog\, today you can’t tell
  who’s a bot\, troll\, or CIA agent.  A notion that can feed into consp
 iratorial thinking and schizoposting. For Paglen\, we are currently moving
  from surveillance capitalism into an era of psyops capitalism\, where our
  behaviour is not only being tracked for profit\, but also manipulated and
  steered. Others are calling this era\, and the online visual culture in i
 ts wake\, “pysop realism”\, pointing to conspiracy minded posts rooted
  in distrust and paranoia.\nWhile we comment\, post\, and scroll our way t
 hrough social media—externalising our thoughts for data capture—the ra
 mpant digitisation and replication of the earth and biological life is qui
 etly taking place. Companies like Quixel\, with over 18\,000 3D assets tha
 t they’ve captured—plants\, rocks\, logs\, coral reefs\, forest floors
 —sell our earth back to us to be used in gaming and other industries. Go
 ogle Maps has led to Google Earth has led to the end-goal of digitally cap
 turing the entire planet in order to create digital twins\, train AI world
  models\, ‘preserve’ habitats and monuments on the brink of collapse\,
  and build predictive systems. Palantir thrives on this data hoarding\, us
 ing it to analyse and create connections within disconnected data sets for
  militarisation and surveillance purposes. In the US\, Flock cameras scan
  around 20 billion license plates every month\, tracking the movement of p
 eople within cities and between states. And surveillance is expanding even
  further\, onto our own bodies\, with Meta selling over 7 million pairs of
  their Ray-Ban smart glasses in 2025\, a huge jump from previous years. Th
 e processes of capturing\, connecting\, and analysing every element on ear
 th is no longer a paranoid delusion\, it’s unfolding in front of our eye
 s. \n📋 Program\n11.00 – 12.30 \n\nWelcome and introduction\nTalk
  by Daniël de Zeeuw\nTalk by Sophie Publig & Mikkel Rørbo\n\n12.30 – 1
 3.15 \n\nLunch break\n\n13.15 – 15.00 \n\nHalf-Earth Socialism: A Pl
 anetary Crisis Game with Francis Tseng\nThe Internet is a Horrible Place a
 nd I’m Here to Make it Worse: A workshop with Noura Tafeche (online host
 )\nAmbitopian Sonic Un-Realism: A workshop with Søren Siebel \n\n15.15
  – 17.00 \n\nConversation with Marina Otero Verzier (online speaker)
 \nOne pixel\, many worlds\, lecture-performance by Emilia Tapprest & Søre
 n Siebel\n\nThroughout the day there will be an exhibition with works by N
 ick Vyssotsky\, Constant Dullaart\, and Tewa Barnosa. \nDuring the lunch b
 reak San Serriffe will have a pop-up bookshop selling publications from t
 he speakers and artists and other books connected to the symposium’s the
 mes. \nPlease note that the activities between 13.15 and 15.00 won’t be
  broadcast on our livestream. Upon arrival at A Lab you can sign up for on
 e of the activities on a first-come-first-served basis. \nFor Noura Tafech
 e’s and Søren Siebel’s workshops\, you will need to bring a laptop wi
 th you. \nPlease note that Noura Tafeche’s workshop includes discussion
  and analysis of implicit violent imagery and representations of rape cult
 ure\, child abuse\, and self-harm. \n📅 Date: Saturday 24 October\n🕗
  Time: 11.00 – 17.00 CEST\n📍 Location: A Lab and livestream on
  The Inbetween\n🎟️ Tickets: Onsite €27\,50/Student €20/Livestrea
 m 7\,50 \n♿ Accessibility \n\nAt the venue\, a wheelchair-accessible ram
 p is available\, as well as a wheelchair lift. The lift needs to be arrang
 ed in advance\, so please email us at info@thehmm.nl\, at least 3 days bef
 ore the event\, to let us know if you will need it. This allows us to make
  sure everything is ready and accessible upon arrival.\nA Lab has an acces
 sible MIVA (wheelchair-accessible) toilet that meets the applicable access
 ibility requirements.\nDuring the event we can provide live closed caption
 ing for those with hearing impairments and disabilities. Please reach out
  to us via info@thehmm.nl—at least 3 days before the event if possible
 —if you are joining on-site and have this access need\, so that we can r
 eserve a seat for you within view of the screen with captions. If you are
  joining online via our livestream\, live captioning will be available as
  one of the streaming modes.\n\n🩵 This programme is kindly supported by
  the Creative Industries Fund NL\, AFK and hosted by A Lab.
URL:https://thehmm.nl/event/symposihmm-%f0%9f%93%a1-capture-control/
CATEGORIES:The Hmm
END:VEVENT
